akr 0.1
project pdf-integrity

record audit.observation.fact-layer-surface-gap/1 : observation {
    title "pdf-read surfaces about a quarter of the M1 inventory"
    state superseded
    statement """
        Measured against the M1 milestone's required inventory, pdf-read's DocumentReport currently reports: open outcome with recovery events rendered as strings, EncryptionInfo (/V, /R, method, and whether the empty user password genuinely validates against /U), XrefHealth (recovery_used, rebuilt, revision_count), per-page PageStatus (index, media_box_present, CompileStatus), AnnotationInventory as counts per /Subtype only, and six FeatureFlags. Everything else M1 names is absent: PDF version and header offset, per-xref-section detail, linearization, /Info and XMP metadata, embedded files, per-annotation object identity, signature dictionaries and ByteRange, page representation classification, and font and image inventories — plus the lineage identifiers and edit markers from @audit.work.edit-provenance-facts. That is roughly a quarter of the inventory by section count, and the twelve missing sections are enumerated in DocumentFacts::pending in crates/pdf-forensics/src/intake.rs.

        Two findings that change the M1 plan's shape. First, pdf-read already implements the structure-only path the encryption decision needs: examine_failed retries pdf_structure::load_structure alone and reads /Encrypt through a security-less snapshot, so a document that will not open still yields xref health and the encryption declaration. No new work is required there. Second, the M1 plan's claim that 'no new parsing work is required for M1' holds, but only in the sense that pdf-read reaches these facts through the existing resolvers — it does not currently report them. Step 2 is therefore surfacing work of real size, not the thin assembly pass the wording suggests.

        Separately, the ecosystem's fixtures cannot express one case M1 needs: an encrypted document under a non-empty user password on a scheme the standard handler supports. encrypted_fixture() is RC4 V2/R3 whose empty user password validates, so it opens unprompted; aes256_encrypted_fixture() is V5/R6, which the handler declines outright, so no password opens it. Until a fixture exists between those two, the password-upgrade half of the encrypted-mode-labelled acceptance check cannot be tested.
        """
    observed_at git:84d2c43a6e1e558876a421b2f0cc3cb82c373d0d
    method manual
    watches [ "crates/pdf-forensics/src/intake.rs" ]
    blocks [ @audit.work.m1-plan ]
}

